      "proof_check": {
        "initial": "Complete valid proof. Every nonsquare perfect power has a representation with base at least 2 and exponent between 3 and floor(log_2 x). Counting representations gives a valid upper bound despite repetitions and inclusion of some squares. Thus E(m^2)=o(m), and A_{m^2}=m+E(m^2), correctly including 1 among the squares. If only finitely many open square gaps were empty, every sufficiently large gap would contribute a distinct nonsquare perfect power, contradicting this estimate. On each empty gap's integer block, A_n is constant. The block has exactly 2m+1 integers and eventually at least k=A_{m^2}, so it contains the required residue modulo the positive integer k. The left square is included and the next square excluded. Infinitely many disjoint eligible blocks give distinct solutions.",
        "final": "Independently complete and valid. The representation bound and its harmless overcounting are explicit. The contradiction establishes infinitely many empty square gaps, rather than merely exhibiting finitely many. Constancy of A_n on the correctly bounded blocks, coverage of every residue class by k consecutive integers, and distinctness across disjoint blocks are all justified. No essential or exposition gap remains."
      }
    },
    "V": {
      "initial_score": 6,
      "final_score": 6,
      "initial_supported": true,
      "final_supported": true,
      "gaps": [],
      "proof_check": {
        "initial": "Complete valid proof. The representation count bounds nonsquare perfect powers by x^(1/3) log_2 x, giving B(M^2)=o(M). Each nonclean block contains a nonsquare perfect power because its only square is its left endpoint. Disjointness makes the contributions distinct, so among M-1 blocks at most o(M) are nonclean; consequently the number of clean blocks is unbounded. On a clean block, k=A_{m^2}=m+B(m^2), including 1, and eventually k<2m+1. The ceiling construction gives an integer n with m^2<=n<m^2+k<=(m+1)^2, hence n lies in the block and A_n=k. Its construction ensures k divides n+2024. Disjoint blocks yield infinitely many distinct solutions.",
        "final": "Independently complete and valid. Overcounting representations is expressly acknowledged. The lower bound M-1-B(M^2) for clean blocks tends to infinity and rigorously proves infinitude. The decomposition of A_{m^2}, eventual size bound for k, ceiling inequalities, inclusion of the left endpoint, exclusion of the next square, divisibility, and distinctness are all correct. No essential or exposition gap remains."
      }
    }
  },
  "source_caveats": [
    "The reference is not needed to validate these complete alternative proofs.",
    "Reference Lemma 1 needs its ratios restricted to indices where x_n>0. Its proof and application otherwise use undefined ratios at initial zero terms. Beginning the first-crossing argument at k_0 repairs this.",
    "The reference's Step 1.3 upper bound fails for n=1,2,3 because its exponent sum is empty although 1 is a perfect power. Restricting that estimate to n>=4 suffices for the argument.",
    "Lemma 2's final displayed comparison is weaker than its stated conclusion, but the preceding expression 1+k/2+k^2/2 directly proves the required inequality."
  ]
